# Is losing worse than winning is good?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What did Kahneman and Tversky's prospect theory find? Why do we judge gains and losses from a reference point?"
next: {"title":"Why does the default win?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/87-why-does-the-default-win.cast"}

## Same money, opposite choices
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Given 1,000
      - Given 2,000
    values:
      - 16
      - 69
    value_labels: true
    value_format:
      suffix: '%'
      decimals: 0
    colors:
      - green
      - red
    y_label: Chose the coin flip (%)
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 100
    reference:
      value: 50
      label: half
    box:
      x: 510
      'y': 150
      w: 460
      h: 480
    label_size: 22
sources:
    - id: kt1979
      title: 'Prospect Theory: An Analysis of Decision under Risk'
      authors: Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky
      year: 1979
      finding: 'Problem 11 (given 1,000: 50% chance of +1,000 vs +500 sure): 16% chose the gamble, N = 70. Problem 12 (given 2,000: 50% chance of −1,000 vs −500 sure): 69% chose the gamble, N = 68. Identical in final states.'
      doi: 10.2307/1914185

    scratch offer1 cites ["kt1979"] work ["You're given 1,000.","A: coin flip for +1,000","B: +500 for sure"] x 250 y 430 font_size 28 hidden true
    scratch offer2 cites ["kt1979"] work ["You're given 2,000.","A: coin flip to lose 1,000","B: −500 for sure"] x 250 y 430 font_size 28 hidden true
    scratch ends work ["Where you end up:","Sure: 1,000 + 500 = 1,500","      2,000 − 500 = 1,500","Flip: 1,000 or 2,000","either way"] x 250 y 430 font_size 28 hidden true
    text refpt "judged from a reference point" #b5482e x 245 y 200 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Same money, opposite choices"

Does losing a hundred dollars hurt more than winning a hundred feels good?
    draw axes reference_1

B: Here's a test from Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky's 1979 paper. You've just been given a thousand. No (@draw offer1_line_2@)w choose: a coin flip for another thousand, (@draw offer1_line_3@) or five hundred for sure.
    draw offer1_box offer1_line_1

    ask "You've been given 1,000. Which do you take?" poll.choices [{"text":"A coin flip for +1,000","share":0.16},{"text":"+500 for sure","share":0.84}] poll.source kt1979 store p after clear stage own voice b
        right: You chose {p}, like {p.share} of their respondents.

Of their seventy respondents, only sixteen percent took the coin flip. Most grabbed the sure five hundred.
    draw bar_1

    erase offer1

B: Version two, for a new group. You've been given two thousand. Now choose: a coin fl (@draw offer2_line_2@)ip that loses a thousand, or lose f (@draw offer2_line_3@)ive hundred for sure.
    draw offer2_box offer2_line_1

    ask "What share chose the coin flip this time? Drag the bar." on bar_2 store g default "30" voice b
        right: Close: {g.true}% took the flip.
        wrong: You said {g}%: it was {g.true}%.

Sixty-nine percent took the coin flip. The majority flipped sides.
    highlight bar_2

B: Fine, but those are two different questions.
    highlight offer2

    erase offer2

Are they? Count where you end up. Take the sure optio (@draw ends_line_2@)n, and either way you walk away  (@draw ends_line_3@)with fifteen hundred.
    draw ends_box ends_line_1

Take the coin flip, and either way you end with a tho (@draw ends_line_5@)usand or two thousand, fifty-fifty.
    draw ends_line_4

Same final money, same odds. A theory that only looks at where you end up says the answers must match.
    highlight bar_1 bar_2

    pause 0.3

B: Unless people don't count where they end up, but how far they've moved, up or down.
    highlight ends effect box

That's prospect theory's first finding: we judge ou (@draw refpt@)tcomes as gains and losses from a reference point.

## The curve with a kink
domain: {"x":[-1000,1000],"y":[-100,40]}
vars: {"L":{"value":2.25,"min":1,"max":3,"step":0.05}}
sources:
    - id: kt1979
      title: 'Prospect Theory: An Analysis of Decision under Risk'
      authors: Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky
      year: 1979
      finding: 'Value is defined on gains and losses from a reference point; concave for gains, convex for losses, steeper for losses: ''losses loom larger than gains''.'
      doi: 10.2307/1914185
    - id: tk1992
      title: 'Advances in Prospect Theory: Cumulative Representation of Uncertainty'
      authors: Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman
      year: 1992
      finding: Median estimates α = β = 0.88 and loss aversion λ = 2.25.

    axes ax y_label "how it feels" ticks.x [-1000,-500,500,1000] ticks.y [] hidden true
    curve zero expr "0" style.opacity 0 hidden true
    dot ref #3d3833 at.x 0 at.y 0 hidden true
    label l_ref "reference point" below-right attach_to ref hidden true
    text t_gain "gains →" #2f6b8f x 880 y 410 font_size 26 hidden true
    text t_loss "← losses" #b5482e x 120 y 410 font_size 26 hidden true
    curve gains thick #2f6b8f expr "sqrt(max(x, 0))" x_from 0 x_to 1000 cites ["kt1979"] hidden true
    label l_gains "gains" above #2f6b8f attach_to gains hidden true
    curve losses thick #b5482e expr "-L*sqrt(max(-x, 0))" x_from -1000 x_to 0 cites ["kt1979"] hidden true
    text note "(bends exaggerated)" #8f887c x 620 y 600 font_size 20 hidden true
    text quote "“losses loom larger than gains”" #b5482e cites ["kt1979"] x 250 y 640 font_size 30 hidden true
    text lam "λ = {L:2}" #b5482e cites ["tk1992"] x 220 y 600 font_size 40 hidden true
    dot pw #2f6b8f at.x 750 at.y 0 hidden true
    label l_pw "+$750" below attach_to pw hidden true
    dot pl #b5482e at.x -500 at.y 0 hidden true
    label l_pl "−$500" above attach_to pl hidden true
    region gbar #87a878 x_from 730 x_to 770 between ["gains","zero"] style.fill #87a878 style.opacity 0.9 hidden true
    region lbar #c96567 x_from -520 x_to -480 between ["zero","losses"] style.fill #c96567 style.opacity 0.9 hidden true
    text refuse "coin flip: refuse" #b5482e x 640 y 380 font_size 34 bind {"style.opacity":"min(1, max(0, (L - 1.2247)*100))"} style.opacity 1 hidden true
    text accept "coin flip: accept" #4f7a3a x 640 y 320 font_size 34 bind {"style.opacity":"1 - min(1, max(0, (L - 1.2247)*100))"} style.opacity 0 hidden true
    scratch mathcard cites ["tk1992"] work [{"tex":"v(x) = x^{\\alpha}\\ \\text{(gains; v: how it feels)}"},{"tex":"v(x) = -\\lambda(-x)^{\\alpha}\\ \\text{(losses)}"},{"tex":"\\alpha \\approx 0.88,\\ \\lambda \\approx 2.25"},{"tex":"\\text{refuse if } \\lambda > 1.5^{\\alpha} \\approx 1.43"}] x 760 y 235 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "The curve with a kink"

So if we count gains and losses, what does the counting look like?
    draw ax zero ref

B: Kahneman and Tversky drew (@draw l_ref@) it as a curve. Across, the change in m (@draw t_gain@)oney: gains to the right, (@draw t_loss@) losses to the left. Up, how it feels.

B: Gains first. The first hundred dollars feels great, the tenth hundred adds less, so the curve bends over.
    draw gains l_gains

    draw note

B: Losses mirror that, but steeper. In their words (@draw quote@), losses loom larger than gains.
    draw losses

How much steeper?
    erase quote

B: In their 1992 follow-up, a loss weighed (@draw lam@) about two and a quarter times an equal gain. That ratio is loss aversion. Call it lambda.

So offer me a coin flip: lose fiv (@draw pl l_pl@)e hundred dollars or win sev (@draw pw l_pw@)en hundred and fifty. On average I come out ahead.

B: But on this curve the lost five hundred hurts more than the seven fifty pleases. So you say no, as  (@draw refuse@)most people do when offered bets like this.
    draw lbar gbar

B: The bends lean you, too. Ben (@highlight gains@)t over among gains, you prefer a sure thing; bent the other way (@highlight losses@) among losses, you'd gamble to dodge a sure loss.

    draw accept

Try it: slide lambda down toward one, where a loss weighs no more than a gain, and watch the refusal turn into a yes.
    animate L 1 duration 3

    explore params ["L"]

    animate L 2.25 duration 1.5

    erase refuse accept

    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"after"}] then after

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq store m default "Skip it" stage own

@math

    erase mathq

In symbols, the curve is a power of the gain on the right, and lambda times a power of the loss on the left.
    draw mathcard_box mathcard_line_1 mathcard_line_2

With their estimates, alpha about 0.88 and lambda about 2.25.
    draw mathcard_line_3

So you turn down the flip whenever lambda is above about 1.4.
    draw mathcard_line_4

    erase mathcard

@after

    erase mathq

    draw refuse accept

So the curve has two feature (@highlight ref effect circle@)s: a kink at the reference point, an (@highlight gains losses@)d a bend on each side of it.

## Move the zero
domain: {"x":[500,3000],"y":[-120,45]}
vars:
    R:
      value: 1000
      min: 1000
      max: 2000
      step: 10
    L:
      value: 2.25
      min: 1
      max: 3
      step: 0.05
    vs:
      expr: sqrt(max(1500 - R, 0)) - L*sqrt(max(R - 1500, 0))
    vg:
      expr: 0.5*(sqrt(max(1000 - R, 0)) - L*sqrt(max(R - 1000, 0))) + 0.5*(sqrt(max(2000 - R, 0)) - L*sqrt(max(R - 2000, 0)))
    pick:
      expr: min(1, max(0, (vg - vs)*20))
sources:
    - id: kt1979
      title: 'Prospect Theory: An Analysis of Decision under Risk'
      authors: Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky
      year: 1979
      finding: 'Problems 11 and 12: identical final outcomes, 84% chose the sure gain when given 1,000; 69% chose the gamble when given 2,000.'
      doi: 10.2307/1914185
    - id: kr2006
      title: A Model of Reference-Dependent Preferences
      authors: Botond Kőszegi and Matthew Rabin
      year: 2006
      finding: The reference point is a person's recent expectations about outcomes.

    axes ax x_label "final money" y_label "how it feels" ticks.x [1000,1500,2000] ticks.y [] hidden true
    curve v thick #3d3833 expr "sqrt(max(x - R, 0)) - L*sqrt(max(R - x, 0))" x_from 500 x_to 3000 hidden true
    curve refline dashed #8f887c x_expr R y_expr t t_from -115 t_to 42 hidden true
    text l_ref "reference point" #8f887c x 275 y 640 font_size 26 bind.x "275 + 0.332*(R - 1000)" hidden true
    dot p1 #8a5fa8 at.on v at.x 1000 hidden true
    dot p2 #2f6b8f at.on v at.x 1500 hidden true
    dot p3 #8a5fa8 at.on v at.x 2000 hidden true
    box opt_sure "Sure: 1,500" #2f6b8f x 805 y 330 width 230 height 64 hidden true
    box opt_flip "Coin flip: 1,000 or 2,000" #8a5fa8 x 805 y 235 width 230 height 84 hidden true
    shape ring_sure #b5482e shape rect x 805 y 330 width 256 height 84 bind {"style.opacity":"1 - pick"} style.opacity 1 style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    shape ring_flip #b5482e shape rect x 805 y 235 width 256 height 104 bind {"style.opacity":"pick"} style.opacity 0 style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    text lam "λ = {L:2}" #b5482e x 805 y 410 font_size 32 hidden true
    text zero_is "zero = what you have,\nor what you expected" #8f887c cites ["kr2006"] x 250 y 580 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Move the zero"

B: That curve has its zero at the reference point. But who decides where the zero sits?
    draw ax v

In the puzzle, the question did. 'You (@draw refline l_ref@)'ve been given a thousand' puts the zero at a thousand.

B: Then both options are gains: fifteen (@draw p2 opt_sure@) hundred for sure, or a flip  (@draw p1 p3 opt_flip@)between a thousand and two thousand. On the bent-over side, the sure thing wins. (@draw ring_sure ring_flip ends@)

    erase ring_sure ring_flip

Now: 'You've been given two thousand.' Slide the zero there, and the same options become losses: five hundred down for sure, or a flip between a thousand down and nothing.
    animate R 2000 duration 3 ghost.of ["v"]

    ask "Now the zero sits at 2,000. Which does the curve pick? Tap it." choose ["opt_sure","opt_flip"] answer opt_flip
        right: The coin flip: yes.
        wrong: Among losses the curve bends the other way: here, the coin flip.

    draw ring_sure ring_flip

B: Among losses the curve bends the other way, so the flip hurts less than the sure loss. The gamble wins, as it did for sixty-nine percent of the respondents.
    highlight opt_flip

Same money, same curve. Only the zero moved.
    highlight refline

    erase v_ghost

B: And outside the lab, the zer (@draw zero_is@)o is usually what you already have, or what you expected to get.

    erase zero_is

Your turn: slide the reference point and lambda, and find where the choice flips.
    draw lam

    explore params ["R","L"]

    animate L 1 R 1500 duration 2

With lambda at one it flips at fifteen hundred. The more loss-averse the curve, the further the zero has to move before it gambles.
    animate R 1510 duration 2.5

    animate L 2.25 R 1600 duration 2.5

So which side of the kink you stand on, and which way you lean, is decided by the zero, not by the final amount.
    highlight v refline

## How big is the kink?
domain: {"x":[0.5,3.5],"y":[0,10]}
sources:
    - id: tk1992
      title: 'Advances in Prospect Theory: Cumulative Representation of Uncertainty'
      authors: Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman
      year: 1992
      finding: Median loss aversion λ = 2.25 (with α = β = 0.88), from a laboratory study.
    - id: pope2011
      title: Is Tiger Woods Loss Averse? Persistent Bias in the Face of Experience, Competition, and High Stakes
      authors: Devin G. Pope and Maurice E. Schweitzer
      year: 2011
      finding: 'In 2.5 million PGA Tour putts (2004–2009), professionals make birdie putts about 2 percentage points less often than par putts of the same distance. American Economic Review 101(1): 129–157.'
    - id: brown2024
      title: Meta-Analysis of Empirical Estimates of Loss Aversion
      authors: Alexander L. Brown, Taisuke Imai, Ferdinand M. Vieider and Colin F. Camerer
      year: 2024
      finding: 'Mean loss aversion coefficient about 1.955 (95% interval 1.82–2.10) across hundreds of estimates. Journal of Economic Literature 62(2): 485–516.'
      doi: 10.1257/jel.20221698
    - id: walasek2024
      title: A meta-analysis of loss aversion in risky contexts
      authors: Lukasz Walasek, Timothy L. Mullett and Neil Stewart
      year: 2024
      finding: 'Pooled λ about 1.31 (95% CI 1.10–1.53) from risky-choice studies. Journal of Economic Psychology 103: 102740.'
    - id: erterev2013
      title: 'On the descriptive value of loss aversion in decisions under risk: Six clarifications'
      authors: Eyal Ert and Ido Erev
      year: 2013
      finding: 'With low-magnitude mixed gambles, choices look risk neutral; loss aversion appears mainly with high stakes and particular framings. Judgment and Decision Making 8(3): 214–235.'
    - id: galrucker2018
      title: 'The Loss of Loss Aversion: Will It Loom Larger Than Its Gain?'
      authors: David Gal and Derek D. Rucker
      year: 2018
      finding: 'Argues the evidence does not show that losses are generally more impactful than gains; many findings attributed to loss aversion (e.g. the endowment effect) can reflect inertia or status-quo preference. Journal of Consumer Psychology 28(3): 497–516.'
      doi: 10.1002/jcpy.1047
    - id: mrkva2020
      title: 'Moderating Loss Aversion: Loss Aversion Has Moderators, But Reports of Its Death Are Greatly Exaggerated'
      authors: Kellen Mrkva, Eric J. Johnson, Simon Gächter and Andreas Herrmann
      year: 2020
      finding: 'Reply to the critics: loss aversion varies with stakes, expertise and context but is robustly present. Journal of Consumer Psychology 30(3).'
    - id: kkt1990
      title: Experimental Tests of the Endowment Effect and the Coase Theorem
      authors: D. Kahneman, J. L. Knetsch, R. H. Thaler
      year: 1990
      finding: Mug owners' median selling price roughly twice the median buying price of non-owners.
      doi: 10.1086/261737

    axes ax x_label "loss aversion λ" cross corner ticks.x [1,1.5,2,2.5] ticks.y [] hidden true
    curve one dashed #8f887c x_expr "1" y_expr t t_from 0 t_to 9.9 hidden true
    text l_one "1: losses weigh like gains" #8f887c at.data [1.598,9.85] font_size 30 hidden true
    dot p_tk #b5482e at.x 2.25 at.y 9 cites ["tk1992"] size 8 hidden true
    text l_tk "Tversky & Kahneman 1992: 2.25" #3d3833 at.data [2.909,9] font_size 30 hidden true
    icon golf size 80 set twemoji x 430 y 330 icon_look picture of golf hidden true
    text t_golf "par putts: +2 points" cites ["pope2011"] x 640 y 330 font_size 32 hidden true
    dot p_meta #2f6b8f at.x 1.96 at.y 7.8 cites ["brown2024"] size 8 hidden true
    text l_meta "meta-analysis 2024: ≈ 2.0" #3d3833 at.data [2.517,7.8] font_size 30 hidden true
    dot p_risky #2f6b8f at.x 1.31 at.y 6.6 cites ["walasek2024"] size 8 hidden true
    text l_risky "risky choices only, 2024: 1.31" #3d3833 at.data [1.989,6.6] font_size 30 hidden true
    dot p_small #8a5fa8 at.x 1 at.y 5.4 cites ["erterev2013"] size 8 hidden true
    text l_small "small stakes: ≈ 1" #3d3833 at.data [1.415,5.4] font_size 30 hidden true
    text gal "Gal & Rucker 2018:\nno general asymmetry?" #b5482e cites ["galrucker2018"] x 640 y 345 font_size 28 hidden true
    icon mug cites ["kkt1990"] size 70 x 470 y 255 icon ["mug","coffee"] icon_look picture of mug hidden true
    text inertia "or inertia?" #b5482e x 640 y 255 font_size 30 hidden true
    text reply "reply: it varies, not vanishes" #2f6b8f cites ["mrkva2020"] x 640 y 195 font_size 24 hidden true
    text c1 "Reference points: mostly agreed" x 640 y 340 font_size 32 hidden true
    text c2 "λ ≈ 2 everywhere: contested" #b5482e x 640 y 285 font_size 32 hidden true
    text sq "zero = what you have → stay put" #3d3833 x 640 y 225 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "How big is the kink?"

B: So is two to one a law of nature?
    draw ax one l_one p_tk l_tk

The evidence for some kink is real. Professional golfers (@draw golf t_golf@) sink putts for par about two percentage points more often than putts for birdie from the same distance.

Par is their reference point, and a missed par feels like a loss.
    highlight t_golf

    erase golf t_golf

And a 2024 meta-analysis, poo (@draw p_meta l_meta@)ling hundreds of estimates, put average loss aversion at about two.

B: But a second 2024 meta-analysi (@draw p_risky l_risky@)s, of risky choices only, found about 1.3. And with small stakes, Eyal Ert and Id (@draw p_small l_small@)o Erev found people close to neutral.

B: In 2018, David Gal and Der (@draw gal@)ek Rucker went further: the evidence, they argued, doesn't show that losses generally loom larger at all.

Even the famous mugs, where owners asked roughly twice what buyers would pay?
    draw mug

B: That could be inertia: people stick with what they have, whether or not giving it up hurts.
    draw inertia

Defenders answer that loss av (@draw reply@)ersion varies with the stakes and the setting, but hasn't vanished.

    erase gal mug inertia reply

How sure are we? That we judge from a r (@draw c1@)eference point: mostly agreed. That losses weigh abou (@draw c2@)t twice as much, everywhere: contested.

    quiz "A shop charges card users $2 more than cash users. Which wording will card users mind more?" voice b
        right: The surcharge: it frames the $2 as a loss from the cash price.
        wrong: Ask where each wording puts the reference point: here, the surcharge.
        + A $2 card surcharge
        * A $2 cash discount
        * Neither: it's the same price

    quiz "Ana bought a share at $50, Ben at $30. Both are now worth $40. Who is likelier to sell now?"
        right: Ben: selling locks in his gain, while selling would make Ana's loss final.
        wrong: Selling makes a loss final, and losses hurt more: here, Ben.
        + Ben, who sits on a gain
        * Ana, who sits on a loss
        * Both equally

    quiz "Two workers each get a $2,000 bonus. Kim expected $3,000; Lee expected nothing. Who feels better?" voice b
        right: Lee: from an expected zero, $2,000 is a gain; for Kim it is a $1,000 shortfall.
        wrong: The reference point can be what you expected: here, Lee.
        + Lee
        * Kim
        * Both the same

So is losing worse than winning is good? Often, and for big stakes clearly. But the sturdiest finding is the reference point: we count from where we stand.
    highlight c1 effect underline

B: And when the reference point is simply what (@draw sq@) you already have, doing nothing starts to look very attractive.

